WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.490
A child is teetering at the edge of a well.

2
00:00:03.490 --> 00:00:11.460
No one is watching you, there is no reward and no punishment — yet your body lunges forward before you decide anything.

3
00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:17.510
Is that half-second proof that a human being is good in essence, or the mask of a well-trained reflex?

4
00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:27.760
Picture a courtyard.

5
00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:33.490
A hot noon hour, a stone well-curb, its rim worn into grooves by years of rope.

6
00:00:33.490 --> 00:00:36.340
The mouth of the well is open.

7
00:00:36.340 --> 00:00:44.310
A little way off, a child who has only just learned to crawl, hands and knees on the ground, is making his way toward that dark circle.

8
00:00:44.310 --> 00:00:47.000
You are someone passing through.

9
00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:49.210
You do not know the child.

10
00:00:49.210 --> 00:00:51.740
You do not know his family.

11
00:00:51.740 --> 00:00:56.990
You have no business in this courtyard; two minutes from now you will walk out and be gone.

12
00:00:56.990 --> 00:00:59.520
And something happens.

13
00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:06.370
Before you can even get a word out, your shoulders tighten, your breath catches, your foot takes a step forward on its own.

14
00:01:06.370 --> 00:01:09.060
You did not think.

15
00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:11.270
You did not weigh anything.

16
00:01:11.270 --> 00:01:13.800
You did no calculation.

17
00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:16.330
Your body moved before you did.

18
00:01:16.330 --> 00:01:22.220
That half-second sits squarely in the middle of one of the most stubborn questions in the history of philosophy.

19
00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:31.630
The man who built this scene was a thinker who lived in China some twenty-three hundred years ago: Mengzi, the name that comes into English as Mencius.

20
00:01:31.630 --> 00:01:37.520
Even the name of the age he lived in tells you something about human nature: the Warring States period.

21
00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:45.650
Small principalities swallowing one another, armies wiping out villages, alliances made in one season and broken in the next.

22
00:01:45.650 --> 00:01:52.660
Which is to say that Mencius defended the goodness of human beings in one of the eras when human beings were killing one another most.

23
00:01:52.660 --> 00:01:54.390
This matters.

24
00:01:54.390 --> 00:01:59.640
Because his claim is not a pure optimism; it is a claim that has seen blood.

25
00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:04.890
Mencius says: in every person there is a heart that cannot bear the suffering of another.

26
00:02:04.890 --> 00:02:09.180
And to prove it he does not launch into a long theory.

27
00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:12.350
He simply describes that well and that child.

28
00:02:12.350 --> 00:02:19.360
Then he moves to the truly ingenious part: one by one, he eliminates what the thing stirring inside you is not.

29
00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:24.130
That flinch is not about getting on good terms with the child's parents.

30
00:02:24.130 --> 00:02:26.180
You do not even know them.

31
00:02:26.180 --> 00:02:31.910
It is not about earning the praise of neighbours and friends, having them say what a fine man he is.

32
00:02:31.910 --> 00:02:34.120
Nobody is watching.

33
00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:42.890
Nor is it because you cannot stand to hear the child scream, because the sound would distress you; there is no scream yet, the child has not fallen.

34
00:02:42.890 --> 00:02:46.540
Mencius closes all three doors of self-interest.

35
00:02:46.540 --> 00:02:52.590
One thing is left: a stirring with no calculation, no motive, nothing covering it.

36
00:02:52.590 --> 00:02:58.960
Here we have to be careful, because what Mencius says is usually taken to be a stronger claim than it is.

37
00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:03.250
He is not saying that every person dives into the well and saves the child.

38
00:03:03.250 --> 00:03:08.820
Maybe you go in, maybe you freeze, maybe you cannot swim and so you only shout.

39
00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:15.350
His subject is not what you do; it is what passes through you in that half-second before calculation arrives.

40
00:03:15.350 --> 00:03:20.440
He offers as evidence not a human being's behaviour, but the first reaction.

41
00:03:20.440 --> 00:03:23.930
So what is that stirring evidence of?

42
00:03:23.930 --> 00:03:26.620
Here Mencius talks like a gardener.

43
00:03:26.620 --> 00:03:31.710
For him that feeling of pity is not humanity itself but the sprout of humanity.

44
00:03:31.710 --> 00:03:43.200
And he counts four such sprouts: the feeling of pity is the sprout of benevolence; the feeling of shame and disgust is the sprout of righteousness; the feeling of deference, of drawing back in respect,

45
00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:49.410
is the sprout of propriety; and the sense that distinguishes right from wrong is the sprout of wisdom.

46
00:03:49.410 --> 00:03:54.020
These are in a person from birth, he says, just like the four limbs.

47
00:03:54.020 --> 00:03:57.670
Anyone who says I cannot do it is crippling himself.

48
00:03:57.670 --> 00:04:02.600
Let us stay a moment on the word sprout, because the whole matter is there.

49
00:04:02.600 --> 00:04:04.970
A sprout is not a tree.

50
00:04:04.970 --> 00:04:08.780
A sprout is not a promise; it is a possibility.

51
00:04:08.780 --> 00:04:14.350
Unwatered it dries; trampled it is crushed; left in shade it dies as a stunted thing.

52
00:04:14.350 --> 00:04:23.920
Mencius knows this perfectly well, and for it he builds a second scene, one less famous than the well but sadder than it: a mountain called Niu Shan, Ox Mountain.

53
00:04:23.920 --> 00:04:27.890
Once, he says, that mountain was covered in forest.

54
00:04:27.890 --> 00:04:30.900
But it stood at the edge of a city.

55
00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:34.550
The axes came every day and cut the trees down.

56
00:04:34.550 --> 00:04:38.040
Herds were let loose and grazed the new shoots.

57
00:04:38.040 --> 00:04:44.730
The dew that fell at night, the tiny sprouts that came up in the morning, vanished into the mouths of the herd that arrived by day.

58
00:04:44.730 --> 00:04:51.100
Now someone looking at that mountain sees the bare rock and says, so there were never any trees on this mountain.

59
00:04:51.100 --> 00:04:54.590
But that was not the mountain's nature.

60
00:04:54.590 --> 00:04:57.760
That was what happened to the mountain.

61
00:04:57.760 --> 00:05:05.570
Mencius's entire thesis about human beings is stretched between these two scenes: the half-second at the well, and the balded mountain.

62
00:05:05.570 --> 00:05:11.140
There is something inside us, but if that something is not protected it becomes invisible.

63
00:05:11.140 --> 00:05:19.750
And yet, before the same century was out, another thinker raised within the same tradition would bring a very hard objection to this gardener's language.

64
00:05:19.750 --> 00:05:22.760
That thinker's name was Xunzi.

65
00:05:22.760 --> 00:05:29.610
He lived a generation after Mencius, belonged to the same Confucian tradition, read the same texts.

66
00:05:29.610 --> 00:05:37.580
And targeting Mencius by name, he wrote this: human nature is bad; goodness is something made afterwards.

67
00:05:37.580 --> 00:05:46.190
That phrase, made afterwards, is covered by a single word in Chinese, and it means done by human hands, brought about by effort.

68
00:05:46.190 --> 00:05:49.520
So Xunzi is not denying goodness.

69
00:05:49.520 --> 00:05:52.530
He is denying that goodness is natural.

70
00:05:52.530 --> 00:05:56.340
For him goodness is not a sprout but a product of craft.

71
00:05:56.340 --> 00:06:03.830
Look where he goes for evidence: a human being is born with a love of gain, and if you leave him alone, quarrelling comes out.

72
00:06:03.830 --> 00:06:08.760
He is born with envy and hatred, and if you leave him alone, harm comes out.

73
00:06:08.760 --> 00:06:14.810
He is born with the desires of eye and ear and palate, and if you leave him alone, excess comes out.

74
00:06:14.810 --> 00:06:20.060
What comes of its own accord when you set your nature free is not order but chaos.

75
00:06:20.060 --> 00:06:24.830
Order appears not when nature is allowed to flow, but when it is resisted.

76
00:06:24.830 --> 00:06:31.200
And to explain this Xunzi chooses the image of a carpenter: a warped board does not straighten itself.

77
00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:37.410
It is steamed, forced into a vise, pressed into a mould, and only after that is it straight.

78
00:06:37.410 --> 00:06:42.660
A dull blade does not sharpen itself; it is drawn across a whetstone.

79
00:06:42.660 --> 00:06:48.710
A person is the same: he is worked by a teacher, by rules, by ritual, by training.

80
00:06:48.710 --> 00:06:52.680
Then he turns Mencius's own weapon against him.

81
00:06:52.680 --> 00:06:55.530
Picture a hungry man, he says.

82
00:06:55.530 --> 00:07:00.940
Food has been set before him, but his father and his elders are at the table.

83
00:07:00.940 --> 00:07:04.590
He does not reach out; he invites them first.

84
00:07:04.590 --> 00:07:07.760
Now, does this behaviour come from nature?

85
00:07:07.760 --> 00:07:09.330
No.

86
00:07:09.330 --> 00:07:11.540
Nature says eat.

87
00:07:11.540 --> 00:07:19.030
This behaviour is done in spite of nature, and it comes from wherever it was learned: from training, from custom, from propriety.

88
00:07:19.030 --> 00:07:25.400
Which means that the acts we consider finest appear precisely in the moments when we do not do what our nature tells us.

89
00:07:25.400 --> 00:07:29.530
The strength of this objection lies in how hard it is to bury.

90
00:07:29.530 --> 00:07:36.380
Because Xunzi asks a cold question: if a human being is so good, then what are the locks on our doors?

91
00:07:36.380 --> 00:07:39.550
What is the receipt we take after a purchase?

92
00:07:39.550 --> 00:07:47.040
What are the cameras at the street corners, the deposit demanded when you rent, the invigilator pacing the room during an exam?

93
00:07:47.040 --> 00:07:52.930
Every inch of our daily life is built on the assumption that the person across from us might not behave properly.

94
00:07:52.930 --> 00:07:58.180
If we truly believed the story of the well, would we lock the doors of our houses?

95
00:07:58.180 --> 00:08:01.510
This line of thought did not stay in China.

96
00:08:01.510 --> 00:08:10.120
Two thousand years later, in England, a man who had lived through the bloody years of a civil war would take hold of the matter from almost the same place.

97
00:08:10.120 --> 00:08:23.690
In the book he published in sixteen fifty-one, Leviathan, Thomas Hobbes describes what life is like when there is no common power over us: the war of every man against every man, and that famous description — solitary,

98
00:08:23.690 --> 00:08:27.500
poor, nasty, brutish, and short.

99
00:08:27.500 --> 00:08:32.750
But Hobbes has to be read carefully; because he is not saying that people are devils.

100
00:08:32.750 --> 00:08:42.160
What he says is more unsettling: in a place where there is no security, even for a person in his right mind the most reasonable move is to strike first.

101
00:08:42.160 --> 00:08:46.770
So the thing that produces evil is not evil hearts but fear.

102
00:08:46.770 --> 00:08:52.340
Even two well-meaning people can shoot one another in an environment where neither can trust the other.

103
00:08:52.340 --> 00:09:00.950
That is the reason Leviathan exists: not because it takes a human being to be bad, but so as not to leave a human being alone with his fear.

104
00:09:00.950 --> 00:09:11.640
A century later, in Königsberg, a philosopher who placed perhaps the greatest confidence in human reason in all of history would touch the same wound with a far more poetic sentence.

105
00:09:11.640 --> 00:09:22.490
In the essay on universal history he wrote in seventeen eighty-four, Immanuel Kant says: out of such crooked wood as a human being is made of, nothing entirely straight can be carved.

106
00:09:22.490 --> 00:09:27.100
This sentence is not an insult; it is an observation about material.

107
00:09:27.100 --> 00:09:34.910
Kant does not consider the human being uncarvable; he considers him a knotty, grainy, cantankerous material.

108
00:09:34.910 --> 00:09:41.280
And precisely for this reason he says there is a need for law, for constitutions, for institutions.

109
00:09:41.280 --> 00:09:44.770
There is no need to write laws for angels.

110
00:09:44.770 --> 00:09:48.580
Now we have two camps, and both of them look right.

111
00:09:48.580 --> 00:09:51.430
One points to that half-second at the well.

112
00:09:51.430 --> 00:09:53.640
The other to the locked door.

113
00:09:53.640 --> 00:10:00.010
And this argument went round and round for centuries inside the pair good or bad.

114
00:10:00.010 --> 00:10:03.020
Until another tradition changed the question.

115
00:10:03.020 --> 00:10:07.310
In Islamic thought the word that circles this argument is fitrah.

116
00:10:07.310 --> 00:10:15.120
It appears in the thirtieth verse of Surah Ar-Rum in the Qur'an: that original constitution upon which the human being was created.

117
00:10:15.120 --> 00:10:23.890
The same idea is in the famous hadith transmitted by Bukhari: every child is born upon fitrah, and then its surroundings make it this or that.

118
00:10:23.890 --> 00:10:28.340
The subtlety of fitrah lies here: fitrah does not mean good.

119
00:10:28.340 --> 00:10:31.030
Nor does it mean blank slate.

120
00:10:31.030 --> 00:10:33.720
Fitrah is an orientation.

121
00:10:33.720 --> 00:10:35.930
Like a tuning.

122
00:10:35.930 --> 00:10:44.860
A human being comes into the world equipped with an inclination toward the truth and toward the good; but this inclination is not a guarantee, it is an initial setting.

123
00:10:44.860 --> 00:10:46.910
It can be spoiled.

124
00:10:46.910 --> 00:10:49.120
It can be silenced.

125
00:10:49.120 --> 00:10:51.170
It can be covered over.

126
00:10:51.170 --> 00:10:58.980
Do you notice that this description stands surprisingly close to Mencius's sprout, and yet takes Xunzi's objection inside itself as well?

127
00:10:58.980 --> 00:11:01.670
There is something given, yes.

128
00:11:01.670 --> 00:11:05.640
But that given thing is not enough on its own.

129
00:11:05.640 --> 00:11:12.810
Al-Farabi puts this point with an almost technical coolness: a human being is born neither virtuous nor vicious.

130
00:11:12.810 --> 00:11:18.860
What comes at birth is a predisposition; in one person toward this, in another toward that.

131
00:11:18.860 --> 00:11:23.150
Virtue is acquired by habit, by repetition, by doing.

132
00:11:23.150 --> 00:11:30.800
Which is to say that your character is not given to you; what is given to you is the direction in which your character will bend more easily.

133
00:11:30.800 --> 00:11:38.770
Ibn Miskawayh, in the work he wrote at the beginning of the eleventh century, The Refinement of Character, takes a step further.

134
00:11:38.770 --> 00:11:44.020
The very title of the book is his thesis: character is something that can be refined.

135
00:11:44.020 --> 00:11:55.190
He opposes the idea, held by certain physicians and philosophers before him, that disposition does not change, that a person is whatever he was born as, and he builds a very plain argument.

136
00:11:55.190 --> 00:11:59.640
If disposition never changed, there would be no such thing as upbringing.

137
00:11:59.640 --> 00:12:06.170
Advice would be nonsense, education a waste of time, raising a child would have no meaning at all.

138
00:12:06.170 --> 00:12:10.140
But we build our whole lives on the assumption that people can change.

139
00:12:10.140 --> 00:12:12.190
Therefore they change.

140
00:12:12.190 --> 00:12:23.200
Al-Ghazali, in the section he devotes to the training of the soul in his great work known as The Revival of the Religious Sciences, knocks at the same door and gives more concrete examples.

141
00:12:23.200 --> 00:12:26.370
We train a wild horse and turn it into a mount.

142
00:12:26.370 --> 00:12:30.820
We accustom a bird of prey until it will settle on the back of a hand.

143
00:12:30.820 --> 00:12:36.550
If we can change an animal's disposition that much, can a human being not change his own at all?

144
00:12:36.550 --> 00:12:47.720
But al-Ghazali adds a warning here, and this warning separates him from cheap optimism: this change is not sudden, it does not happen by leaping, it is a long and tedious business of habituation;

145
00:12:47.720 --> 00:12:51.530
and it does not proceed at the same speed in everyone.

146
00:12:51.530 --> 00:12:56.940
Some dispositions soften with a slight effort; some resist for a lifetime.

147
00:12:56.940 --> 00:13:02.670
Centuries later, this same line meets us in the laboratory in an entirely different language.

148
00:13:02.670 --> 00:13:13.680
In studies in developmental psychology, it has been shown many times over that children of only eighteen months, who cannot yet speak properly, will spontaneously help an adult who drops what he is holding,

149
00:13:13.680 --> 00:13:17.810
going to pick the object up and hand it over with no reward promised.

150
00:13:17.810 --> 00:13:28.340
In puppet experiments with even younger infants, there are findings that babies prefer the character who helps over the character who hinders; the debate in this second area is still going on,

151
00:13:28.340 --> 00:13:33.590
but the first findings stand uncomfortably close to Mencius's intuition about the sprout.

152
00:13:33.590 --> 00:13:38.040
And right here, the same science turns over the other face of the coin.

153
00:13:38.040 --> 00:13:46.170
In an experiment carried out at the theology department of Princeton University in America, students were asked to give a short talk in another building.

154
00:13:46.170 --> 00:13:52.540
Some of them were told they would be speaking on the famous parable of the good-hearted stranger who helps the injured man by the road.

155
00:13:52.540 --> 00:13:55.710
So that subject was in their heads exactly.

156
00:13:55.710 --> 00:14:00.960
Then some were told hurry, you are late, and others were told you have plenty of time.

157
00:14:00.960 --> 00:14:07.650
And along the way, someone was placed in a doorway, coughing and groaning, appearing to be in need of help.

158
00:14:07.650 --> 00:14:13.700
The result is this: sixty-three percent of those who were not in a hurry stopped and attended to him.

159
00:14:13.700 --> 00:14:17.350
Of those told they were late, only ten percent.

160
00:14:17.350 --> 00:14:22.920
Some of them stepped over the man in need of help in order to make it to a talk about helpfulness.

161
00:14:22.920 --> 00:14:26.730
This experiment does not refute Mencius.

162
00:14:26.730 --> 00:14:30.540
It adds a piece of ground-level information to him.

163
00:14:30.540 --> 00:14:38.350
That half-second at the well may be real; but in order to hear that half-second, a person's life has to contain that half-second of space.

164
00:14:38.350 --> 00:14:48.880
Haste, fear, exhaustion, crowds, the fact that responsibility has been spread among others — all of these are more than enough to suppress that stirring inside us.

165
00:14:48.880 --> 00:14:54.130
The sprout may be standing there; but remember, too, Mencius's balded mountain.

166
00:14:54.130 --> 00:14:58.580
If the herd passes by every day, no one can see what grew there.

167
00:14:58.580 --> 00:15:01.910
So the answer is neither angel nor monster.

168
00:15:01.910 --> 00:15:10.040
The answer is that a human being is born with a predisposition, and that predisposition grows or withers according to what is built around it.

169
00:15:10.040 --> 00:15:19.450
And this stops being a philosophical refinement and turns into an extremely practical question: if the environment is this decisive, who builds the environment?

170
00:15:19.450 --> 00:15:23.580
This is where the real price of the argument about human nature is paid.

171
00:15:23.580 --> 00:15:27.870
Because the answer you give to this question does not stay inside your head.

172
00:15:27.870 --> 00:15:35.040
It founds schools, writes laws, collects taxes, runs companies, raises children.

173
00:15:35.040 --> 00:15:37.570
Mencius says this quite openly.

174
00:15:37.570 --> 00:15:43.300
A ruler who has a heart that cannot bear the suffering of the people builds a government that cannot bear it either.

175
00:15:43.300 --> 00:15:54.950
The things he defended standing in front of kings were not abstract virtues: do not take the people's fields from them, do not tear the peasant away from his field in the season of war, fill people's bellies first,

176
00:15:54.950 --> 00:15:57.480
education comes after that.

177
00:15:57.480 --> 00:16:02.570
He went so far as to hold that deposing a ruler who crushes his people was legitimate.

178
00:16:02.570 --> 00:16:07.980
Because for him the foundation of politics was the ability to grow that feeling at the well.

179
00:16:07.980 --> 00:16:11.470
Xunzi's followers arrived somewhere else entirely.

180
00:16:11.470 --> 00:16:15.920
If a human being is a warped board, who will hold the vise?

181
00:16:15.920 --> 00:16:22.130
Xunzi had thought of this vise as ritual, custom and education; he was a Confucian to the end.

182
00:16:22.130 --> 00:16:32.340
But two students raised alongside him, Han Fei and Li Si, drew a far harsher conclusion from the same premise: people are brought into line not by training but by punishment.

183
00:16:32.340 --> 00:16:42.230
Li Si became chief minister of the Qin dynasty, which unified China for the first time, and that dynasty went down in history for its severe laws and heavy penalties.

184
00:16:42.230 --> 00:16:47.640
Out of the same diagnosis, an order far more merciless than the teacher's was born.

185
00:16:47.640 --> 00:16:52.570
Ibn Khaldun, in the Muqaddimah, looks at the matter with a historian's eye.

186
00:16:52.570 --> 00:17:00.220
It is necessary for human beings to live together, he says; alone a man can neither feed himself nor protect himself.

187
00:17:00.220 --> 00:17:05.950
But when they come together, the tendency toward aggression and injustice inside them comes with them.

188
00:17:05.950 --> 00:17:12.800
For this reason every community needs a restrainer to hold people back from one another — that is, an authority.

189
00:17:12.800 --> 00:17:17.570
Ibn Khaldun writes this not as wishful thinking but as an observed law.

190
00:17:17.570 --> 00:17:24.900
And the man who writes this also describes how civilisation produces the finest human skills, arts and sciences.

191
00:17:24.900 --> 00:17:29.350
His human being, too, is not one-sided.

192
00:17:29.350 --> 00:17:34.920
Now let us bring this argument into our own day, because something very clear happens there.

193
00:17:34.920 --> 00:17:41.290
A study carried out in ten daycare centres in Israel gave what may be the most striking evidence on this question.

194
00:17:41.290 --> 00:17:47.020
Some parents were late picking up their children, and the teachers were forced to wait past their hours.

195
00:17:47.020 --> 00:17:51.150
The centres imposed a small fine for lateness.

196
00:17:51.150 --> 00:17:54.800
What was expected was that the fine would reduce the lateness.

197
00:17:54.800 --> 00:17:59.890
What happened was the exact opposite: lateness increased, roughly doubling.

198
00:17:59.890 --> 00:18:02.420
The reason was this.

199
00:18:02.420 --> 00:18:08.630
Before the fine, being late was a disgrace; you were keeping a teacher waiting, you were embarrassed.

200
00:18:08.630 --> 00:18:13.240
After the fine, being late became a service, a thing with a price.

201
00:18:13.240 --> 00:18:16.730
I am paying for it, so I am comfortable.

202
00:18:16.730 --> 00:18:20.060
The most unsettling part came afterwards.

203
00:18:20.060 --> 00:18:24.830
When the fine was removed, lateness did not go back to its old low level.

204
00:18:24.830 --> 00:18:33.120
That is: an institution used an instrument because it assumed people were self-interested, and that instrument actually made people more self-interested.

205
00:18:33.120 --> 00:18:35.170
And permanently.

206
00:18:35.170 --> 00:18:39.140
This is a far smaller scene than any grand theory of politics.

207
00:18:39.140 --> 00:18:42.310
But that is exactly why it is instructive.

208
00:18:42.310 --> 00:18:45.800
Because all of us design at this scale every day.

209
00:18:45.800 --> 00:18:57.770
A manager who assumes his employees will slack off installs cameras, brings in a sign-in book, counts minutes; and after a while he finds in his hands a team that really does no more than fill out its minutes.

210
00:18:57.770 --> 00:19:04.780
An examination system that assumes the student will cheat turns the student into someone trying less to learn than to avoid being caught.

211
00:19:04.780 --> 00:19:16.910
The reverse is true as well: on some roads in Anatolia there are still stalls with nobody standing over them, vegetables laid out on top, a tin can beside them; you leave the money, take the goods and go.

212
00:19:16.910 --> 00:19:25.360
That stall makes a type of human being possible in the place where it is set up, and that human being, more often than not, really does appear.

213
00:19:25.360 --> 00:19:31.410
So the real matter is not to answer the question is a human being good from birth once and then shelve it.

214
00:19:31.410 --> 00:19:36.820
We answer this question every day; not with words, but with the things we build.

215
00:19:36.820 --> 00:19:43.830
With the regulation we write, the penalty we impose, the look that makes plain whether or not we trust our own child.

216
00:19:43.830 --> 00:19:48.920
And right here, that child at the well takes on another meaning.

217
00:19:48.920 --> 00:19:53.850
In Mencius's scene there is a child waiting to be saved, yes.

218
00:19:53.850 --> 00:19:59.100
But in that scene there is also an adult who notices something stirring inside himself.

219
00:19:59.100 --> 00:20:03.070
Perhaps the real question is not whether the child was saved.

220
00:20:03.070 --> 00:20:08.640
The real question is whether the adult is living inside a life in which he can hear that stirring.

221
00:20:08.640 --> 00:20:16.290
If you build a world that takes that half-second away from a person, then you will never be able to find out whether a human being is good from birth.

222
00:20:16.290 --> 00:20:20.580
Nöron Philosophy'de bugünkü bölümün sonuna geldik.

223
00:20:20.580 --> 00:20:24.390
Yeni bir hikâyede yeniden buluşmak üzere.
